---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.7 — Informal Run-Time Analysis &amp; The RuntimeAnalyzer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.12 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> On the AP Computer Science A
    exam, informal run-time analysis measures algorithm speed not with a stopwatch (which varies by
    CPU), but by counting <strong>how many times a key statement executes</strong> as the input size
    <code>n</code> grows. Today you will design and build <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code> to
    instrument linear, logarithmic, and quadratic algorithms and compare their scaling side by side.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/RuntimeAnalyzer.java" download>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</a> — starter file
            with Javadocs, stubs, and a built-in <strong>Algorithm Scaling Benchmark Table</strong>
            in <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions on counter
            placement, binary search midpoint updates (avoiding infinite loops!), and why skipping
            half a grid still scales quadratically.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; benchmark in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, and inspect the
            live scaling table with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac RuntimeAnalyzer.java
java RuntimeAnalyzer</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once your benchmark table matches your scaling
        predictions, unlock <code>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>12 checks</strong> on your first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Linear Search Instrumentation</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countLinearSearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A linear search checks elements one by one from index <code>0</code> to
            <code>n - 1</code>. By instrumenting a step counter inside the loop, we can see exact
            best-case (<code>1</code> step), average-case (<code>n / 2</code>), and worst-case
            (<code>n</code>) performance.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int n</code> — number of items (indices <code>0 .. n - 1</code>).</li>
            <li><code>int targetIndex</code> — target index being sought (may lie outside
                <code>0 .. n - 1</code> to simulate a missing item).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> total comparisons performed before
                finding <code>targetIndex</code> or finishing all <code>n</code> indices.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(1000, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>1</code> step (best case: found immediately).</li>
            <li><code>(1000, 499)</code> &rarr; <code>500</code> steps.</li>
            <li><code>(1000, 999)</code> &rarr; <code>1000</code> steps; <code>(1000, -1)</code>
                &rarr; <code>1000</code> steps (not found).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countLinearSearchSteps</code></h3>
        <p>Plan the loop and counter placement before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Counter Placement:</strong> Should you use a
                <code>for</code> loop or a <code>while</code> loop here, and why? Inside the loop
                body, why must <code>steps++</code> execute <em>before</em>
                <code>if (i == targetIndex) return steps;</code> (what would
                <code>(1000, 0)</code> return if you put <code>steps++</code> after the
                <code>if</code>)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countLinearSearchSteps</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, and check the Linear column in
                <code>java RuntimeAnalyzer</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Binary Search Halving &amp; Logarithmic Scaling</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>countBinarySearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            When data is sorted, <strong>binary search</strong> inspects the midpoint
            <code>mid = (lo + hi) / 2</code> and discards half of the remaining range on every
            comparison. Instrumenting binary search lets you watch logarithmic ($O(\log_2 n)$)
            growth crush linear ($O(n)$) search as <code>n</code> doubles.
        </p>
        <h4>Algorithm Rules, Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>Initialize <code>int lo = 0;</code>, <code>int hi = n - 1;</code>, and
                <code>int steps = 0;</code>.</li>
            <li>While <code>lo &lt;= hi</code>: increment <code>steps</code>, compute
                <code>int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;</code>, return <code>steps</code> if
                <code>mid == targetIndex</code>, set <code>lo = mid + 1</code> if
                <code>mid &lt; targetIndex</code>, or set <code>hi = mid - 1</code> otherwise.</li>
            <li>If the loop exits without finding <code>targetIndex</code>, return
                <code>steps</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(1000, 499)</code> &rarr; first <code>mid = (0 + 999) / 2 = 499</code> &rarr;
                <code>1</code> step!</li>
            <li><code>(15, 14)</code> &rarr; <code>mid</code> visits <code>7 &rarr; 11 &rarr; 13 &rarr; 14</code>
                &rarr; <code>4</code> steps.</li>
            <li><code>(15, 99)</code> &rarr; visits <code>7 &rarr; 11 &rarr; 13 &rarr; 14</code>,
                then <code>lo = 15 &gt; hi = 14</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> steps.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countBinarySearchSteps</code></h3>
        <p>Compare linear vs. binary search design and midpoint bounds:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Scaling Comparison:</strong> Why does binary search require a
                <code>while</code> loop instead of a fixed-bound <code>for</code> loop? Why does
                doubling <code>n</code> from <code>1023</code> to <code>2047</code> add
                <code>1024</code> worst-case steps to linear search, yet only <strong><code>1</code></strong>
                step to binary search?</li>
            <li><strong>Infinite-Loop Edge Case (<code>mid + 1</code> / <code>mid - 1</code>):</strong>
                Trace <code>countBinarySearchSteps(15, 99)</code> when
                <code>lo == 14, hi == 14, mid == 14</code>: what happens if a programmer writes
                <code>lo = mid;</code> instead of <code>lo = mid + 1;</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countBinarySearchSteps</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, and compare the Linear vs. Binary columns in
                <code>java RuntimeAnalyzer</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Quadratic Growth: Full Grid vs. Unique Pairs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> &amp; <code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Students often ask whether optimizing a nested loop from <code>j = 0</code> to
            <code>j = i + 1</code> (like <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code> in Lesson 4.4!) changes
            its big-picture growth rate. Instrumenting both shapes side by side proves how
            <code>10&times;</code> larger input scales the workload.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> — counts inner steps for
                <code>0 &lt;= i &lt; n</code> and <code>0 &lt;= j &lt; n</code> (<code>0</code> if
                <code>n &lt;= 0</code>).</li>
            <li><code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code> — counts inner steps for
                <code>0 &lt;= i &lt; n</code> and <code>i + 1 &lt;= j &lt; n</code> (<code>0</code> if
                <code>n &lt;= 1</code>).</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>n = 10</code> &rarr; Full Grid: <code>100</code>; Unique Pairs:
                <code>45</code> (<code>10 * 9 / 2</code>).</li>
            <li><code>n = 1</code> &rarr; Full Grid: <code>1</code>; Unique Pairs: <code>0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: Quadratic Nested Loops</h3>
        <p>Compare the two nested shapes before writing them:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Boundary &amp; Comparison:</strong> How do the loop conditions handle
                <code>n = 0</code> and <code>n = 1</code> automatically without extra <code>if</code>
                guards, and why does <code>countUniquePairSteps(1)</code> return <code>0</code>
                while <code>countFullGridSteps(1)</code> returns <code>1</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Scaling Factor Prediction:</strong> In
                <code>countUniquePairSteps</code>, going from <code>n = 100</code> (<code>4,950</code>)
                to <code>n = 1000</code> (<code>499,500</code>) multiplies <code>n</code> by
                <code>10&times;</code>—by roughly what factor do the steps grow, and why does cutting
                a quadratic loop in half still leave it quadratic ($O(n^2)$)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write both <code>countFullGridSteps</code> and <code>countUniquePairSteps</code>,
                copy them into <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code>, and verify the scaling table in
                <code>java RuntimeAnalyzer</code> before unlocking <code>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java</code>
                below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods populate the scaling benchmark table in <code>java RuntimeAnalyzer</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>12 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/run-time-analysis.html">Run-Time Analysis</a> — linear, quadratic, and logarithmic step counting</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.7" title="4.7 Informal Run-Time Analysis &amp; RuntimeAnalyzer Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
